{"reply":"To find the probability, we first need to identify all the two-digit numbers whose squares end with the digit 1. A number's square ends with 1 if and only if the number itself ends with 1 or 9. 

Considering the set of all positive two-digit integers (10 to 99), the numbers that end with 1 or 9 are 11, 19, 21, 29, ..., 91, 99. We notice that in every set of ten consecutive numbers, exactly two numbers satisfy this condition (e.g., 11 and 19 between 10 and 19). Since there are 90 two-digit numbers (from 10 to 99), and we can divide them into nine sets of ten consecutive numbers, we have a total of 9 * 2 = 18 numbers that end with 1 or 9.

Therefore, the probability that the selected number's square will end with the digit 1 is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (18) to the total number of outcomes (90). This can be calculated as 18/90, which simplifies to 1/5. So, the probability is 1/5 or 0.2.","device_command":null}

